---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Picture from '../components/Picture.astro';
import {selected} from '../data/catalog';
import {url} from '../lib/urls';
const path='/about/';
---
<Layout title="About the workshop" path={path}>
  <section class="page-title about-hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">About Cubey</span><h1>Following the<br/> next idea.</h1><p class="intro">Cubey is my personal workshop for graphics and simulation. I work with coding agents to explore the things I want to understand, or just want to see running.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/demos/',path)}>See what I have been making ↗</a></div><figure class="workshop-montage"><div><Picture id={selected('ocean-surface').poster} path={path} alt="Ocean waves" eager/></div><div class="square"><Picture id={selected('guardian').poster} path={path} alt="The authored Guardian sculpture" eager/></div><figcaption>Ocean and Guardian · two corners of the workshop.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section class="section about-story"><div><span class="eyebrow">Where it started</span><h2>An old engine,<br/> a wider playground.</h2></div><div class="body-copy"><p>Cubey began as a way to revive an old engine and return to graphics experiments. My earlier work was about learning C++ and OpenGL, building a small foundation and trying what I could at the time.</p><p>The scope has grown considerably with coding agents. Some topics were familiar, like glTF and fluid simulation. Others were things I wanted to try: ocean, atmosphere, clouds, terrain. Fluid simulation branched in several directions; procedural terrain became a much deeper rabbit hole.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section" id="shared-foundation"><div class="section-head"><h2>The foundation grows with the experiments.</h2><p>A useful result can change what becomes possible elsewhere.</p></div><div class="feedback-loop" role="group" aria-label="Experiments and the shared foundation support each other"><div><span class="eyebrow">Experiments</span><h3>Try something interesting.</h3><p>A scene puts a technique to work and exposes what it needs.</p></div><div class="feedback-arrows" aria-hidden="true"><span>⇄</span><small>ideas move both ways</small></div><div><span class="eyebrow">Shared foundation</span><h3>Build on what proves useful.</h3><p>Rendering, assets, lighting and materials support the next experiment.</p></div></div><div class="grid two connection-notes"><article><h3>Water experiments meet terrain.</h3><p>The 2.5D fluid work fed into the terrain-water experience. Shared terrain rendering carries useful changes between the projects.</p><p class="note">The featured Terrain Water film replays recorded SynxFlow fields. The builtin shallow-water study remains a separate demo.</p><div class="scene-links"><a href={url('/demos/simulation/shallow-water/',path)}>Shallow water ↗</a><a href={url('/demos/terrain/#terrain-water',path)}>Terrain Water ↗</a></div></article><article><h3>A sky becomes a light source.</h3><p>Atmosphere and clouds became more than a standalone view. Their shared lighting and backdrop also support the material workshops.</p><div class="scene-links"><a href={url('/demos/atmosphere/',path)}>Atmosphere &amp; light ↗</a><a href={url('/demos/materials/',path)}>Material workshops ↗</a></div></article></div></section>
  <section class="section about-story"><div><span class="eyebrow">How I work</span><h2>Built with coding agents.</h2></div><div class="body-copy"><p>I choose what to pursue and review where it goes. Coding agents make it possible to build a wider foundation, explore more techniques and spend more time refining the results.</p><p>The aim is to understand and implement established ideas carefully. That means checking assumptions, comparing results and being clear about the limits. Sometimes an approach needs a fresh start; sometimes it gets cut. Those decisions are part of the work too.</p><p>What I follow next is still an open question. For now, this site collects the experiments worth showing.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('/demos/',path)}>Explore the demos ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="section about-story"><h2>Look closer.</h2><div class="body-copy"><p>The source, build guides and technical notes are in the repository. The current native projects use C++ and Vulkan; OpenGL belongs to the earlier learning project.</p><div class="scene-links"><a href="https://github.com/byebyebryan/cubey">Source repository ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/byebyebryan/cubey/blob/main/docs/getting-started.md">Build and run ↗</a><a href={url('/credits/',path)}>Media and credits ↗</a></div></div></section>
</Layout>
